{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And the judges shall inquire diligently, and behold, the witness is a false witness</b>. Scripture did not explain how it becomes known that he is a false witness, for since the matter involves two witnesses who testify about a thing, even if a hundred come and contradict them, it would not be clarified that they answered falsely. Nor can we say that the murdered man came on his own feet [i.e., he was alive], for in that case it would not be stated, 'and the judges shall inquire diligently.' Therefore the trusted tradition came and explained that the disqualification (הֲזָמָה) occurs when they say, 'But on such-and-such a day you were with us' (Makkot 5a). The reason is that this testimony concerns the very persons of the witnesses, and they are not believed regarding themselves to say, 'We did not do such-and-such,' since those others can say about them that they murdered a person or desecrated the Sabbath."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:51:53.388905929Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}